 To make the circle element look more 3D, you can add some additional CSS properties such as a higher contrast gradient and a transformation to give the illusion of depth. Here's an updated version of your `.circle` class:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(at bottom, #fdf1cd 0%, #fde6cf 50%, #d9ead3 100%); /* Added a radial gradient with a yellowish hue at the bottom */
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(-15px -15px 15px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)); /* Added a negative drop-shadow for depth effect */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75), inset 1px 1px 5px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); /* Added a white inset box-shadow for highlight effect */
  border-radius: 50%;
  transform: translateZ(5px); /* Added a slight z-index offset for extra depth perception */
}
```

Keep in mind that these changes might not have a significant impact on the actual 3D appearance of the circle if it is displayed as a vector graphic or SVG. For more advanced 3D effects, consider using WebGL or Three.js libraries instead.

