Helpfulness (97): Suppose you have an Angular application, and you want to fetch data from an API using HttpClient. To do this, first, you need to import `HttpClient` from `@angular/common/http`. Then, inject it into your component or service by adding it as a constructor argument:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = 'myApp';
  data: any;

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
}
```

Next, create a method to call the API and assign the response to a property in your component:

```typescript
getData() {
  this.http.get('/api/data').subscribe((response: any) => {
    this.data = response;
  });
}
```

Finally, call the method when your component initializes:

```typescript
ngOnInit() {
  this.getData();
}
```

Now, whenever `ngOnInit` runs, your Angular application will make a request to the specified API endpoint and store the response in the `data` property.

