 To call a PowerShell script that requires user input (read using `Read-Host`) from Terraform, you cannot directly use Terraform's built-in `local-exec` or `remote-exec` provisioners as they don't support interactive input. Instead, consider the following workarounds:

1. Pass variables as arguments to your PowerShell script and set them in Terraform before running the script. This way, you can avoid using `Read-Host` for inputs that don't require user interaction.

```hcl
provider "powershell" {
  version = "3.0.0"
}

output "script_output" {
  value = "${powershell.example.stdout}"
}

resource "local_file" "myfile" {
  content = jsonencode({
    myVar = "MyValue"
  })
}

data "template_file" "ps_script" {
  template = file("path/to/your/powershell/script.ps1")

  vars = {
    myVar = local_file.myfile.content
  }
}

output "ps_script" {
  value = data.template_file.ps_script.rendered
}

resource "powershell_script" "run_script" {
  input = jsondecode(data.template_file.ps_script.rendered)

  inline = [
    "& ${join("\r\n", [
      "Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Force", # Temporarily change execution policy for this script only
      data.template_file.ps_script.rendered,
    ])}"
  ]
}
```

Replace `path/to/your/powershell/script.ps1`, `MyValue`, and update your PowerShell script accordingly.

2. Use external scripts without `Read-Host`: Refactor your PowerShell script to accept command line arguments instead of using `Read-Host`. Then, pass those arguments when calling the script using `local-exec` or `remote-exec` provisioner.

3. Use PowerCLI or other tools like Pester, PSExec, or Invoke-Command to run your PowerShell script with required arguments

