 Creating a haiku using C# code and generating random lines that fit the structure of a traditional haiku (5-7-5 syllables) about a lone tree in a misty meadow is quite complex. Instead, I'd suggest creating predefined strings for each line and randomly selecting them to create the illusion of a unique haiku. Here's an example:

C# Code:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string[] firstLine = { "Lone tree stands tall", "Tree in misty meadow" };
        string[] secondLine = { "Silent whispers dance", "Mist veils its ancient form" };
        string[] thirdLine = { "Peaceful solitude reigns", "Nature's symphony plays on" };

        Random rnd = new Random();
        int[] indices = new int[3] { 0, 0, 0 }; // initialize with zeros

        while (indices[0] == indices[1] || indices[1] == indices[2] || indices[2] == indices[0])
        {
            indices[0] = rnd.Next(firstLine.Length);
            indices[1] = rnd.Next(secondLine.Length);
            indices[2] = rnd.Next(thirdLine.Length);
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"Haiku:\n{firstLine[indices[0]]}\n{secondLine[indices[1]]}\n{thirdLine[indices[2]]}");
    }
}
```

