 To create a function in Swift that rearranges the letters in a given phrase and returns a new phrase reflecting a mindset of optimism and gratitude, you can modify the existing function as follows:
```swift
func rearrangePhrase(_ phrase: String) -> String {
    let sortedLetters = phrase.filter { !$0.isWhitespace }.sorted()
    let positiveWords = ["hope", "love", "gratitude", "optimistic"]
    var newPhrase = ""

    for word in positiveWords {
        if phrase.contains(word) {
            let indexes = phrase.startIndexes(of: word)!
            let randomIndex = Int.random(in: 0..<indexes.count)
            let substring = phrase[indexes[randomIndex]...]
            newPhrase += "\(String(sortedLetters))\(substring)"
            phrase.removeSubrange(indexes[randomIndex]..<phrase.endIndex)
            break
        }
    }

    if newPhrase.isEmpty {
        newPhrase = "Life is worth living with \(String(sortedLetters))!"
    }

    return newPhrase
}
```
This modified function uses an array of positive words and looks for each one in the input phrase. If it finds a match, it randomly selects an occurrence of the word, moves it to the end of the new phrase, and keeps the sorted letters in the middle. If no positive word is found, it falls back to the original behavior of returning the sorted letters at the end of the phrase.

Calling this function with the example phrase would produce a result like:
```swift
